    "lawCode": "Amd §2799-gg, ren §2799-uu to be §2799-vv, add §2799-uu, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8418\n\nSPONSOR: KRUEGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to authorizing\nadditional bonds of the New York city transitional finance authority\nrelated to the epidemic resulting from spread of the disease known as\nCOVID-19 disaster emergency\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill authorizes the New York City Transitional Finance Authority\n\n(the \"NYCTFA\") to issue bonds in an amount outstanding of up to seven\nbillion dollars for costs, including lost revenues, associated with the\nCOVID-19 epidemic. This amount would be in addition to any other amounts\nauthorized by applicable law. Section two of the bill would add a new\nsection 2799-uu that would give the NYCTFA this authority.\n\nSection one of the bill would make corresponding amendments to subdivi-\nsion 1 of section 2799-gg of the Public Authorities Law to refer to the\nissuance of bonds by the NYCTFA beyond its current borrowing limita-\ntions, consistent with new section 2799-uu. Section one would also\nconsolidate into one sentence this additional exception and the existing\nexceptions set forth in section 2799-gg.  Other than adding the conform-\ning reference to the additional borrowing for COVID-19 costs, this bill\ndoes not alter the NYCTFA's existing borrowing capacity.\n\nSection three of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nThe City of New York (the \"City\") is the epicenter of the nation's\nCOVID-19 epidemic, having recorded a higher number of infections than\nChina. The aggressive social distancing measures necessary to contain\nCOVID-19 severely disrupted economic activity in the City starting in\nMarch 2020. The disruption included a halt to non-essential businesses\nand a sharp decline in employment in the City. In its Executive Budget\nfor fiscal year 2021, the City projected a $7.4 billion loss in tax\nrevenues in its fiscal years 2020 and 2021. The City has spent over $700\nmillion to fight COVID-19 and is expecting to spend approximately $3.5\nbillion by the end of calendar year 2020.  Due to the severe and sudden\neconomic impact of COVID-19, it is necessary to provide the City with\nthe ability to finance its unprecedented expenses and losses in revenues\nover time in order to allow the City to continue providing essential\nservices to its residents.\n\nThe New York City Transitional Finance Authority (the \"NYCTFA\") was\npreviously authorized by State law to borrow for costs related to and\narising from the terrorist attack on the City on September 11, 2001,\nincluding lost revenues. Permitting the NYCTFA to borrow for such costs\nresulting from the COVID-19 epidemic will provide the City with funding\nneeded to maintain public services critical to fighting the spread of\nCOVID-19 and to mitigate the economic impact on City taxpayers by\nspreading the costs of the epidemic over time.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable consider-\nation of this proposal by the Legislature.  Respectfully submitted,\n",
